text-right whitespace-nowrap">$20,760</td><td class="border-b border-line px-2.5 py-2 text-right whitespace-nowrap">$42,350</td><td class="border-b border-line px-2.5 py-2 text-right whitespace-nowrap">$64,804</td><td class="border-b border-line px-2.5 py-2 text-right whitespace-nowrap">$88,157</td><td class="border-b border-line px-2.5 py-2 text-right whitespace-nowrap">$112,443</td></tr><tr class="bg-paper-2 font-bold"><td class="border-b border-line px-2.5 py-2 text-left whitespace-normal">Property ahead (behind)</td><td class="border-b border-line px-2.5 py-2 text-right whitespace-nowrap text-loss">-$32,869</td><td class="border-b border-line px-2.5 py-2 text-right whitespace-nowrap text-loss">-$12,727</td><td class="border-b border-line px-2.5 py-2 text-right whitespace-nowrap">$2,180</td><td class="border-b border-line px-2.5 py-2 text-right whitespace-nowrap">$18,078</td><td class="border-b border-line px-2.5 py-2 text-right whitespace-nowrap">$34,589</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class="mx-5 mb-5 mt-4 list-disc pl-[18px] text-[13px] text-muted max-[600px]:mx-4"><li class="mb-1"><strong>Cap rate</strong> = a full rented year of net operating income ÷ purchase price.</li><li class="mb-1"><strong>Net equity</strong> = property value − selling costs − loan balance.</li><li class="mb-1"><strong>Total return</strong> = after-tax cash flow to date + net equity − tax on the sale + tax saved by held losses − cash invested.</li><li class="mb-1"><strong>Tax on the sale</strong> is charged on the gain: sale price − selling costs − (purchase price + closing costs + renovation − depreciation taken). The part of the gain that comes from depreciation is taxed at your bracket, capped at 25%; the rest at your long-term gains rate. State tax and the 3.8% net investment income tax are not included, and a loss on sale is shown as no tax.</li><li class="mb-1"><strong>Same cash invested elsewhere</strong> = what your cash invested would have earned at 4% a year, compounded yearly, if you had put all of it in on day one.</li><li class="mb-1"><strong>Income tax</strong> = (net operating income − mortgage interest − depreciation) × your tax bracket. A loss saves no tax that year. It is held, offsets later rental profit first, and whatever is left is used when you sell.</li><li class="mb-1"><strong>Depreciation</strong> starts once the property is ready to rent, so year 1 counts only the months after renovation.</li><li>While the property is being renovated there is no rent, but property tax, insurance and HOA dues still count.</li></ul></details><p class="mx-1 mt-5 max-w-[90ch] text-[13px] text-muted">This is a preliminary estimate based on the numbers you entered and the assumptions shown. It is not tax, legal or investment advice and is not a guarantee of return. Talk with your tax and investment advisors before you buy.</p><p class="mx-1 mt-2 max-w-[90ch] text-[13px] text-muted">Tenant disclaimer for this test</p></section><a href="#reo-results" class="fixed inset-x-3 bottom-3 z-10 hidden items-center gap-[18px] rounded-lg bg-green-900 px-3.5 py-2.5 text-white shadow-pop max-[980px]:flex print:hidden"><span class="flex min-w-0 flex-col"><span class="text-[11px] text-on-dark-2">5-year return</span><strong class="text-[17px] tabular-nums">$147,032</strong></span><span class="flex min-w-0 flex-col"><span class="text-[11px] text-on-dark-2">Monthly cash flow</span><strong class="text-[17px] tabular-nums">$2,166</strong></span><span class="ml-auto whitespace-nowrap text-[13px] font-bold text-mint">See results</span></a>"`;
